{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "הגר אשר בקרבך", :translit "ha-ger asher b'kirbecha", :gloss "the stranger who is in your midst"} {:he "כי ימוך אחיך ונמכר לגר", :translit "ki yamuch achicha v'namakar la-ger", :gloss "when your brother becomes poor and is sold to a stranger"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Verbal link: both verses use the word 'ger' (stranger)"}], :frame {:text "Leviticus 25:47 describes the scenario in which a person becomes so impoverished that he must sell himself to a 'ger toshav' (resident stranger) living among the Israelites. The Ba'al HaTurim links the curse in Deuteronomy 28:43 — where the stranger rises higher and higher while Israel sinks lower — to this legal context of servitude to a stranger.", :sources ["Leviticus 25:47" "Deuteronomy 28:43"]}} {:quotes [{:he "מטה מטה", :translit "mateh mateh", :gloss "lower lower"} {:he "גיהנם", :translit "geihinnom", :gloss "Gehenna"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "מטה מטה", :b "גיהנם"}], :frame {:text "Deuteronomy 28:43 warns that the stranger among Israel will rise 'higher and higher' while Israel descends 'lower and lower' (מטה מטה). Gehenna is described in rabbinic literature as a place of punishment and purification; for example, the wicked are judged in Gehenna (Eruvin 19a). The Ba'al HaTurim's gematria equates the doubled 'lower and lower' with 'Gehenna,' indicating that Israel's descent is not merely social degradation but a spiritual fall toward damnation.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 28:43" "Eruvin 19a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Verbal link: both verses use the word 'ger' (stranger)", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "מטה מטה", :a-value 108, :b "גיהנם", :b-value 108, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:40:09.367763296Z", :model "glm-5.2"}